7 platforms + SHA256SUMS) as evidence and confirm the README's asset\nnames match byte-for-byte (`boardctl_${goos}_${goarch}`).\n\nACCEPTANCE CRITERIA\n- the reusable workflow ref is a tag or a SHA (not @main) and the workflow\n  still runs green on a push to main and on a tag\n- a hermetic check (Go test or a CI step) FAILS when the Makefile platform\n  list and the `platforms:` input diverge\n- the published asset set for the current release is captured as evidence\n  and matches README's names (7 platforms + SHA256SUMS)\n- the pin changes no release CONTENT beyond the ref itself; if moving the pin\n  changes the build, that is a FINDING to report, never a silent fix","detail":{"fingerprint":"22e5a33e4501bf2ab7b5aa82bddc55f63f74bde0197fa7db140c7d30db4a1069"},"complexity":2,"updated_at":"2026-09-24T11:06:39.900675+00:00","capability_tags":["+infra","+release"],"worker_status":"pending","commit_hash":null,"guard_result":null,"ci_result":null,"worker_summary":null,"foreman_note":null,"completed_at":null,"sessions":null,"worktree":"/home/kara/worktrees/coding-hermes-boardctl-BT-052","branch":"wt/BT-052"}
 {"id":"BT-053","title":"Roll the board-lint pre-commit hook out to EVERY fleet repo including PAUSED projects, and file the install row in each project's own board","status":"pending","priority":"P1","type":null,"created_at":"2026-09-23T07:12:38.218814+00:00","created_by":null,"reasoning":"WHY THIS ROW EXISTS\nAn installed hook protects one repo. The fleet has 96 repos under /home/kara\nwith a .git dir, 71 of which already carry the GitReins pre-commit hook. The\noperator's instruction (2026-09-23) is explicit: put the task in ALL other\nprojects INCLUDING PAUSED ones, so the work happens by itself rather than\nbeing retried by hand — \"this way you don't need to try it, they will\".\n\nDEPENDS ON BT-051. Do not start before `boardctl install` exists: the thing\nrolled out must be the TESTED installer, not a hand-copied shell snippet, or\nevery repo ends up with a slightly different hook — the exact drift this row\nis meant to prevent.\n\nDELIVERABLE\n1. A fleet rollout command (a `--fleet <glob|list>` mode of `boardctl\n   install`, or a thin script looping the per-repo installer) that:\n   - enumerates the code repos (with and WITHOUT a board — the installer's\n     own skip path is part of what must be tested)\n   - installs idempotently, CHAINS an existing hook, never clobbers, and\n     prints a per-repo outcome line (installed / already present /\n     skipped-no-board / skipped-no-binary / FAILED + why)\n   - is safe to re-run and safe to run while ticks are live\n2. A row filed in EVERY planet's board naming this rollout, so coverage is\n   visible where the work happens — INCLUDING the PAUSED projects\n   (temple-runner, rabbit-hole, ring-runner, asce, musterflow,\n   escalation-doctrine, Kobayashi-Maru and the paused h3 family). Paused\n   foremen will never run, so the SCRIPT is what installs and the ROW is what\n   records: both halves are required.\n3. A re-runnable census proving coverage: N repos scanned, N hook present,\n   N chained-with-gitreins, N skipped and WHY, plus a list of any repo that\n   could not be installed with its reason. This census is the row's evidence.\n\nACCEPTANCE CRITERIA\n- every repo in the census is either installed (hook present AND chained) or\n  skipped with a named, correct reason — no silent misses\n- a repo carrying the GitReins hook still has it afterwards, and BOTH hooks\n  still fire\n- PAUSED projects appear in both the census and the filed rows\n- a SECOND rollout run reports \"already present\" for every repo and changes\n  nothing (idempotence proven, not asserted)\n- the census output is pasted into the row as evidence\n\nSEQUENCING: BT-051 -> this row. Do not run this in the same wave as BT-051;\nthe installer must be merged and released first.","detail":{"fingerprint":"dfc2f6c9453e42e57aafda95a88bc3bd72c6808e0ae814a0f9398e988e003b1f"},"complexity":2,"updated_at":"2026-09-23T07:12:38.218814+00:00","capability_tags":["+infra","+dx"],"worker_status":"pending","commit_hash":null,"guard_result":null,"ci_result":null,"worker_summary":null,"foreman_note":null,"completed_at":null,"sessions":null,"depends_on":["BT-054"]}
 {"id":"BT-054","title":"boardctl install: a git pre-commit hook that REJECTS a bad board (duplicate id / dangling ref), chained with the existing GitReins hook and bounded by a 30s timeout that SKIPS instead of blocking","status":"complete","priority":"P1","type":null,"created_at":"2026-09-23T07:13:07.143459+00:00","created_by":null,"reasoning":"WHAT AND WHY\nA user who clones boardctl from GitHub, or runs it in a fleet repo, gets the\ntool but NOT the protection: a board carrying a duplicate id or a dangling\ndepends_on reference can still be committed, and the damage only surfaces\nlater when a reader chokes on it. `boardctl validate` already detects every\none of these classes — nothing runs it at the moment a bad board would land.\nThis row is about TIMING, not detection.\n\nEVIDENCE (2026-09-23, measured)\n- `boardctl validate` on a healthy board: \"RESULT: OK (0 warning(s))\", exit 0.\n  On the board damaged by the canopy incident it returned\n  `[error] tasks.jsonl line 386: duplicate task id \"DF-HERMES-CANOPY-52\"\n  (also line 382)` -> \"RESULT: FAIL (1 error, 35 warnings)\", exit 1.\n  The detector exists, works, and names line numbers.\n- BT-050 (open, P2) documents the writers that bypass `boardctl create`, and\n  create ALREADY refuses a duplicate id (exit 2). So the guard is complete;\n  it simply never runs at commit time.\n- Fleet census 2026-09-23: **71 of 96** repos under /home/kara already carry\n  `.git/hooks/pre-commit` — the GitReins Tier-1 hook, generated by\n  `gitreins install`, whose binary path is PINNED at install time and which\n  ends in a bare `exit $?`. Git allows exactly ONE pre-commit file, so a\n  naive installer either clobbers that guard or gets clobbered by it.\n  CHAINING IS THE CORE CONSTRAINT OF THIS ROW, not an option.\n\nDELIVERABLE (tests-first per repo doctrine, with a RED ledger entry)\n1. `boardctl hook run` — the hook's logic as a GO VERB, so it is testable\n   rather than shell:\n   - resolves the board like the sibling verbs (-C / repo root / board dir).\n   - SKIPS CLEANLY (exit 0, one-line notice) when: not a board repo; the\n     board file is absent; the boardctl binary cannot be resolved; or the\n     staged set contains no board file (`--always` lints unconditionally).\n     A hook that fails a commit because the tool is missing is worse than\n     no hook at all.\n   - STRICT lint on the staged board: duplicate id, dangling depends_on\n     reference, unparseable line, out-of-vocabulary status -> FAIL (exit 1,\n     naming the line numbers and the offending ids). The softer classes\n     (priority vocabulary, legacy warnings) stay WARN and do NOT block.\n   - BOUNDED: `--timeout` default **30s**; an expiry is exit 0 plus a loud\n     SKIP notice. Rationale: the box may be saturated with running agents or\n     the board may be large, and a lint hook must never wedge a commit.\n   - Escape hatches, documented rather than hidden: `BOARDCTL_HOOK_SKIP=1`\n     and `git commit --no-verify`.\n2. `boardctl install [--hook] [--uninstall] [--force] [--global]`:\n   - CHAINS: detect an existing pre-commit (the GitReins hook, or anything\n     else), preserve it verbatim, and install a dispatcher that runs each\n     hook in order and aggregates exit codes. The board-lint step runs FIRST\n     so it reports before the slower guard. Never overwrite without --force.\n   - `--global`: install once into a shared directory and point\n     `core.hooksPath` at it, so a machine covers future clones; the per-repo\n     install stays available.\n   - Idempotent and reversible: a re-run is a no-op, `--uninstall` restores\n     the previous hook byte-for-byte.\n3. The \"easy to add to repos\" bundle: an example script under `examples/`,\n   a README section showing `boardctl install` in three lines, and a SKILL\n   that agents load stating the hook contract (what blocks, what warns, what\n   skips) so a foreman that hits a rejection knows the REPAIR path instead of\n   reflexively reaching for --no-verify.\n4. A fleet-rollout mode for BT-053 to consume: install across many repos,\n   report a per-repo outcome, never clobber.\n\nACCEPTANCE CRITERIA\n- a duplicate id in the staged board -> `git commit` is REJECTED; the message\n  names the id and both line numbers; `--no-verify` still works\n- a dangling depends_on reference -> the same rejection path\n- a clean board -> the commit proceeds unchanged\n- no board / no binary / no board file staged -> exit 0, skip notice, commit\n  proceeds\n- lint exceeding the timeout (forced via a 1s `--timeout`) -> exit 0 + SKIP\n  notice, commit proceeds\n- in a repo carrying the GitReins hook, after install BOTH hooks run (prove\n  the gitreins guard still blocks when its own config says it should), and\n  `--uninstall` restores the original hook byte-identically\n- re-running `boardctl install` is a no-op (no duplicated block, no diff)\n- hermetic tests (no network, no real gitreins) pin every line above\n\nSCOPE NOTE: the vocabulary of which validate classes are errors lives in\n`validate` and must stay in ONE place — this row consumes it, never forks it.","detail":{"fingerprint":"c9f11396ef662174ca5b1387e35480d58867ec682e01523c8bd214380ae9ac0c"},"complexity":3,"updated_at":"2026-09-23T11:11:57.891526+00:00","capability_tags":["+infra","+dx","+go"],"worker_status":"complete","commit_hash":"c74a76a5215f2b338acd757ebec2f0b14ba72101","guard_result":"PASS","ci_result":"GREEN","worker_summary":"boardctl install: board-lint pre-commit hook (wave worker 5cebc4f + tier2 remediation 4a40798, merged c74a76a). Judge FAIL->PASS after remediation: PASS verdicts .gitreins/history/2026-09-23/53c9153b/verdict.json (remediated tree) and FAIL .gitreins/history/2026-09-23/60b15ee7/verdict.json (original, kept for audit). Findings fixed: (1) dangling depends_on now rejected by the hook via new 'boardctl validate --fail-on dangling-dep' (plain validate stays warn/exit-0); (2) chaining rewritten so board-lint runs after the existing hook body with early exits neutralized (existing-hook reject and lint reject both block). Foreman live-probed: duplicate id => hook exit 1; re-install x2 => single managed block; timeout-skip verified.","foreman_note":"Wave worker glm-5.3-flash@zai-glm; worktrees /home/kara/worktrees/coding-hermes-boardctl-BT-054 branch wt/BT-054. Tier-2 judge caught dangling-ref warn-only + inverted chain order; remediation round landed both.","completed_at":"2026-09-23T11:11:41Z","sessions":["20260923_050845_cf1f18","20260923_053446_daecef"],"depends_on":[],"worktree":"/home/kara/worktrees/coding-hermes-boardctl-BT-054","branch":"wt/BT-054"}
-{"id":"BT-055","title":"validate silently yields nil for a non-array depends_on, so a dependency expressed in the wrong shape is invisible (BT-035 carries the string \"[]\")","status":"pending","priority":"P3","type":null,"created_at":"2026-09-23T07:14:55.096843+00:00","created_by":null,"reasoning":"FOUND 2026-09-23 while filing BT-052/053/054 (the board-lint pre-commit hook work),\nso it belongs to that family rather than to a new investigation.\n\nEVIDENCE\n- Exactly ONE row on this board has a depends_on that is not an array: BT-035,\n  which carries the STRING \"[]\" instead of an empty array. Every other row is\n  either a real array or the key is absent.\n- The tolerance is deliberate and documented in prose:\n  internal/board/validate.go:220 — \"depends_on). Non-array / malformed values\n  yield nil.\" — and validate.go:171 feeds rowStringSlice(row, \"depends_on\") into\n  the existence cross-check. A non-array therefore yields NO dependencies, the\n  cross-check has nothing to test, and `boardctl validate` reports\n  \"RESULT: OK (0 warning(s))\" for BT-035.\n- The WRITE side is strict: internal/board/write.go:141 types DependsOn as\n  []string, so boardctl's own writer could never emit this shape. This is\n  legacy or hand-written data, exactly the population BT-050 describes.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS (the class, not the single row)\nThe whole point of BT-054's hook is to stop a board carrying bad data from being\ncommitted. A dependency expressed as a string is bad data that today round-trips\nas \"no dependencies\" — silently. The failure is invisible in precisely the case\nthat hurts: if a row used the malformed shape with a REAL reference\n(\"depends_on\": \"BT-019\"), validate would emit no dangling-ref warning and a\nreader would never see the edge at all. Tolerating the shape on READ is\ndefensible; tolerating it SILENTLY is not.\n\nDELIVERABLE (small)\nAdd the class to `validate` (or to BT-054's strict lint) as a WARN:\n\"tasks.jsonl line N (task ID): depends_on is not an array — treated as no\ndependencies\". WARN and not error, so it cannot block a commit on legacy data by\nitself; BT-054's hook owns the exit policy.\n\nACCEPTANCE CRITERIA\n- `boardctl validate` emits the new warning for BT-035 and still exits 0\n- correcting BT-035 to [] makes the warning disappear\n- a row with a real-but-malformed ref (\"depends_on\": \"BT-019\") is caught by the\n  SAME warning — that is the case that actually protects a dependency edge\n- no change to the existing dangling-ref check (BT-007 behaviour) and no new\n  error introduced on any other row in this repo","detail":{"fingerprint":"b32c1b4ffde136838fde887bedcb5b825f33a664b55f6efb0f3115fed3c72b63"},"complexity":1,"updated_at":"2026-09-23T07:14:55.096843+00:00","capability_tags":["+dx","+validator"],"worker_status":"pending","commit_hash":null,"guard_result":null,"ci_result":null,"worker_summary":null,"foreman_note":null,"completed_at":null,"sessions":null,"depends_on":[]}
