 {"id":"BT-056","title":"[P2] Declare the task-row key canon and report key-uniformity drift in validate (implementation complete)","status":"complete","priority":"P2","type":null,"created_at":"2026-09-23T07:55:15.245175+00:00","created_by":null,"reasoning":"MEASURED 2026-09-23 (fleet review programme, subgoal 2). Before this change validate's only row-shape test was 'the line parsed'. The census over 57 fleet boards / 8,422 parsed rows found ~230 distinct keys: one-key dialects (why on 19 rows, all one project), composite squatters (lines_added_removed), and outright typos (review_otes on 2 rows). Nothing reported any of it. DESIGN: the canon is DECLARED IN CODE (internal/board/keys.go), not in a JSON/TOML manifest — the boards are consumed by this package, so the canon belongs with the code that reads them, and a data file would let a board widen its own canon. Tiers: RequiredTaskRowKeys {id,title,status,priority}; SanctionedTaskRowKeys = DefaultTaskRowKeys UNION the measured extras (computed, so a change to the create schema can never silently become drift); and the `_note` suffix rule, which sanctions an annotation FAMILY so each new annotation does not need a canon edit. TaskKeyAliases is the separate table of spellings that are drift WITH a named repair. CHAINS: validate always prints the census line 'key uniformity: N/M rows carry only canon keys'; drift is a warning by default (a legacy board must still validate OK) and --strict-keys promotes it to an error. `update --normalize` now also renames aliases, and it REFUSES rather than guesses when the canonical key is already on the row (two values, one slot) or the offending key is duplicated — RenameKey preserves the key's POSITION, so the rewritten line differs only in the spelling and the value bytes are untouched. EVIDENCE (gitreins, the criterion's board): before 309/329 rows canonical, drift 20 rows {why(19), acceptance(1)}, all fixable; --strict-keys exited 1. After 'update --normalize' on those 20 ids: 'key uniformity: 329/329 rows carry only canon keys (0 drift)', exit 0 with AND without --strict-keys. The rewrite was proven structural, not just textual: 329 -> 329 lines, exactly 20 changed, each one key rename with the value preserved and every other key byte-identical. TESTS: internal/board/keys_test.go covers both directions (canon membership, the _note family, a clean-board 0-drift census, per-row drift findings naming the fix path, RenameKey position/value preservation, all three refusals, and an idempotent end-to-end normalize that leaves an untouched row byte-identical). Full repo suite green. The DF-9 usage contract test was EXTENDED (not weakened) to assert the new flag and the census line are documented. NOTE for the foreman: ParseRow already refuses duplicate keys, so the duplicate-source branch in RenameKey can only be reached by constructing a Row directly — it stays as a guard on a public mutable method and is tested that way.","detail":{"fingerprint":"e55f2b65f06fd33bc2b71638188d1d2feeac547dd86d02a4686daf1364172fda"},"complexity":3,"updated_at":"2026-09-23T07:55:15.265249+00:00","capability_tags":[],"worker_status":"pending","commit_hash":null,"guard_result":null,"ci_result":null,"worker_summary":null,"foreman_note":"IMPLEMENTED AND VERIFIED 2026-09-23. Artifact chain: internal/board/keys.go (canon) + internal/board/keys_test.go (both directions + refusals) + the validate census line + update --normalize alias repair + --strict-keys. Zero-drift proof on the criterion board: \"key uniformity: 329/329 rows carry only canon keys (0 drift)\", validate exit 0 with and without --strict-keys, and the 20-row repair proven structural (20 changed lines, one key rename each, every other key byte-identical). Cannot close REVIEW-GITREINS-015 from here: that row lives on the gitreins board.","completed_at":null,"sessions":null,"depends_on":[]}
 {"id":"BT-057","title":"[P1] The deployed boardctl was ~3 days stale and hid 96 validate findings — add a deployment freshness gate","status":"pending","priority":"P1","type":null,"created_at":"2026-09-23T07:55:15.255449+00:00","created_by":null,"reasoning":"FOUND 2026-09-23 (fleet review programme, subgoal 2) while proving the new validate check end-to-end. The boardctl actually on PATH (/home/kara/.local/bin/boardctl) was built from revision 79a10123 (2026-09-20T06:07:33Z, mod v0.1.7-0.20260920060733) while the repo HEAD was f3834174 (2026-09-23T07:15:10Z, mod v0.1.9-0.20260923071510) — roughly three days of committed checks were missing from every validate run the fleet performed in that window. HOW IT SURFACED: the same board was validated with both binaries. The stale one reported 'RESULT: OK (1 warning(s))' on gitreins; the current source reports OK (97 warnings): 96 of them are the BT-048 out-of-vocabulary-priority warnings that the deployed build predates. So any validate exit-code/warning count quoted from a fleet tick in that window under-reports findings — including the numbers this review programme itself recorded for other boards. Live values behind those 96: gitreins carries priority 'low' (48 rows), 'high' (26), 'medium' (22) and a bare integer (2) alongside the canonical P0-P3 — the write path normalizes bare 0-3 and case variants but not these prose values, so they persist. WHERE DID THEY COME FROM is a separate question worth a row: the board importer claims to normalize 'completed' -> 'complete' and priority to P0-P3, so an import path may be the writer. FIX APPLIED NOW, but not the durable fix: the current build was installed (vcs.revision f3834174) and gitreins re-validated at 329/329 key uniformity / exit 0. DURABLE FIX (this row): nothing catches a stale deployment — boardctl ships internal/versioncheck, so check whether a deployment-freshness gate belongs there (compare the running binary's embedded vcs.revision against the repo HEAD and warn loudly), and wire it where the fleet builds the tool. Same class as the bunker PATH-binary staleness (GAP-143) and the dagger build stamp (subgoal 5): the installed binary, not the source tree, is what produces the evidence.","detail":{"fingerprint":"f695eafeb6d906372c991af4fa4895cd0753bbed3b5175ad0aea6e1897822167"},"complexity":3,"updated_at":"2026-09-23T07:55:15.255449+00:00","capability_tags":[],"worker_status":"pending","commit_hash":null,"guard_result":null,"ci_result":null,"worker_summary":null,"foreman_note":null,"completed_at":null,"sessions":null,"depends_on":[]}
 {"id":"BT-058","title":"P2 — the key-alias table was audited against real rows and four candidates were removed as concept-merging (plus a counter that over-reported repairs)","status":"complete","priority":"P2","type":null,"created_at":"2026-09-23T09:02:45.546507+00:00","created_by":null,"reasoning":"FOUND 2026-09-23 while auditing my own instrument rather than the boards. BT-056 shipped a TaskKeyAliases table; an alias is a REPAIR INSTRUCTION that --normalize executes, so a wrong entry silently merges two concepts on every board that carries the key. Checked all 11 candidates against 57 boards / 8,443 parsed rows, comparing rows that carry BOTH the alias and its target. FOUR REMOVED: (1) commit -> commit_hash: 6 rows carry it and TWO carry commit_hash with a DIFFERENT value (commit=afffb64 vs commit_hash=9b4536e on crier CI-001) - two different commits, so the rename would have rewritten which-commit on a row that legitimately says two things; (2) deps -> depends_on: 7 rows, every value the EMPTY STRING and no depends_on anywhere - the rename puts a string where the canon expects a list of ids; (3) review_otes -> review_notes: 2 rows, 100% conflict, and the values are DIFFERENT KINDS - review_otes holds captured cell output as prose while review_notes on the same rows holds provenance notes (id-renumbered records) - THIS ALSO CORRECTS BT-056, whose text calls review_otes an outright typo of review_notes; (4) review_notes_dupe: a candidate I invented that matched no row at all. KEPT, now with evidence recorded in the code: why->reasoning, acceptance/pass_criteria/_pass_criteria/ac->acceptance_criteria (_pass_criteria: 222 rows, 0 conflicts, values are genuine pass conditions such as 'PASS: grep -r ... == 0'), pri->priority, cpx->complexity. SECOND FIX: the census and the per-row finding counted a row as fixable whenever every offending key was an alias, but RenameKey REFUSES when the canonical key is already present - including present as null - so it advertised repairs that then failed. A row is now fixable only if every offending key is a known alias AND its target is ABSENT. MEASURED EFFECT: bunker 2 fixable -> 0 (its review_otes rows are no longer sold as a mechanical fix), off-by-one keeps 10 genuine ones, gitreins unaffected at 329/329 zero drift. Regression test TestAliasWithPresentTargetIsNotAdvertisedAsFixable covers both directions. LESSON worth keeping: the alias table is the one part of this canon that WRITES, so it is held to sample evidence, not to plausibility - a plausible abbreviation ('commit', 'deps') was wrong twice out of four.","detail":{"fingerprint":"d0b88c4f41dd49d9dac666c417b0a8666f9b5d551cc934c9997201e7c8f38dda"},"complexity":3,"updated_at":"2026-09-23T09:02:45.556684+00:00","capability_tags":[],"worker_status":"pending","commit_hash":null,"guard_result":null,"ci_result":null,"worker_summary":null,"foreman_note":"IMPLEMENTED + VERIFIED 2026-09-23 (commit e37e1a1, pushed): four aliases removed, the fixability rule tightened, the comment block in internal/board/keys.go records the per-entry evidence, and the new regression test passes. Full repo suite green. Board: exit 0. Verified live after redeploy: gitreins 329/329 (0 drift), bunker 0 fixable (was 2), off-by-one 10 fixable, and a NULL-target row (pri beside priority:null) no longer advertises --normalize.","completed_at":null,"sessions":null,"depends_on":[]}
-{"id":"BT-059","title":"P2 — create's key schema is computed from the board's own rows, so a drifted board makes every NEW row inherit its drift (observed: repo:null emitted on SCHED-GAP-1575)","status":"pending","priority":"P2","type":null,"created_at":"2026-09-23T17:04:54.907902+00:00","created_by":null,"reasoning":"OBSERVED 2026-09-23 while filing a P1 on the scheduler board. boardctl create SCHED-GAP-1575 emitted a row containing repo:null, and validate then flagged it: 'key(s) {repo} outside the declared canon'. Nothing in my create call asked for repo. The mechanism is by design and is the problem: the create schema is the create-time default set UNION the extras MEASURED FROM THAT BOARD's existing rows (the same union that feeds the key-uniformity census), so on a board that already carries drift, every new row written through the tool inherits that drift and validates with a warning. Compare GAP-145 created the same way on the bunker board minutes earlier: no repo key, because bunker's rows do not carry one. WHY IT MATTERS: the tool exists to STOP drift spreading; a write path that mirrors the drift it is measuring makes the census self-perpetuating - repairing the old rows does not stop new ones being born dirty, and a board can never reach zero drift while any legacy row carries an extra key. It also weakens the census as evidence: 'N rows drift' partly counts rows the tool itself created. SUGGESTED FIX: separate the two sets explicitly - keep a canonical create schema (what a new row SHOULD carry) apart from the measured census (what rows DO carry), and have create write only the canonical schema, leaving the census to report the difference. If a board's extra key is deliberate, it belongs in the canon (like labels, sanctioned 2026-09-23) or in the alias table - not silently inherited. ALSO SEEN while checking, filed here rather than as its own row because it is the same class: that board's SECOND validator error, 'board.jsonl line 1: EOF', is a FORMAT error - the file is pretty-printed JSON spanning many lines while the validator reads JSONL (one object per line), so line 1 can never parse. Both that error and 'duplicate task id INT-CI-006' are pre-existing (verified against the committed board: same 2 errors, 287 warnings), i.e. this row added none.","detail":{"fingerprint":"b93b8e5bb6f60dfbe9f55681bf54bbd15a112b8769f48748d8810b8d3184d65e"},"complexity":3,"updated_at":"2026-09-23T17:04:54.907902+00:00","capability_tags":[],"worker_status":"pending","commit_hash":null,"guard_result":null,"ci_result":null,"worker_summary":null,"foreman_note":null,"completed_at":null,"sessions":null,"depends_on":[]}
